    "lawCode": "Add §97-yyyy, St Fin L; amd §212, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6780\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the state finance law, in relation\nto establishing the board of regents institutional accreditation\naccount to provide the framework for the state education department to\nspend monies received and/or revenues generated for institutional\naccreditation purposes; and to amend the education law, in relation to\nservices and functions relating to institutional accreditation\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to establish the framework needed, provided\nthe enactment of an appropriation, to allow the Department to spend\nmonies received, including appropriations, revenues and/or other\nmonies received for institutional accreditation purposes, to support\nthe Department's accreditation function including the addition of\ndedicated accreditation staff, and expenses incurred by the Department\nin order to support and enhance conducting accreditation activities\nfor institutions of higher education in this State.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill adds a new section 97-YYYY to the State Finance\nLaw to establish the \"Board of Regents institutional accreditation\naccount\" to allow the Department to receive for deposit to the credit\nof such account, revenues generated pursuant to regulations of the\ncommissioner of education for services and expenses incurred by the\nstate education department in conducting accreditation of institutions\nof higher education or monies appropriated or otherwise provided to\nthe state education department for such purposes.\n\nSection 2 of the bill adds a new subdivision 7 to section 212 of the\neducation law to clarify the Department's authority to collect\nrevenues from institutions of higher education that seek to be\naccredited by the Department.\n\nSection 3 is the effective date.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT OF THE BILL :\n\nThe Regents have been engaged in the evaluation of quality in higher\neducation in New York since 1787. The federal government has\nrecognized the Regents and the Commissioner as a national\ninstitutional accrediting agency since 1952. New York is the only\nstate with this recognition. Currently, 14 New York colleges and\nuniversities hold institutional accreditation by the Regents.\n\nInstitutional accreditors must meet the federal government standards\nfor accrediting agencies and ensure that accreditation and\naccreditation renewal processes and decisions are made consistent with\nthose standards. These processes, which include review of voluminous\ndocuments and extensive site visits to the institutions, are time and\nresource intensive for the accrediting agencies.\n\nOther institutional accrediting agencies in this region (e.g., the\nMiddle States Commission on Higher Education or \"Middle States\")\nsupport their functions by charging their participating institutions.\nFor example, Middle States charges its participating institutions both\nannual dues, calculated on the institution's total educational and\ngeneral expenditures, as well as fees for various\naccreditation-related activities (information session fees,\napplication fees, site visit fees and expenses, etc.).\n\nCurrently the Department does not charge for institutional\naccreditation. While there are many benefits of this accreditation\nfunction carried out by the Office of Higher Education through its\nOffice of College and University Evaluation, accreditation activities\ndivert limited financial and staff resources from other important\nfunctions (e.g., program registration and institutional oversight.)\n\nThe bill would provide for the establishment of an account to allow\nthe Department to collect and spend monies received for accreditation\npurposes, provided an appropriation, to support its accreditation\nfunction, including the addition of dedicated accreditation staff, and\nexpenses incurred to support and enhance the accreditation of\ninstitutions of higher education in this State. An appropriation would\nalso be needed by the Legislature and Executive, starting in the\n2018-19 budget and beyond, to make up the difference between the\nmonies collected under this bill and the $650,000 that the Department\nneeds to hire the staff, which would need to be approved by the\nDivision of Budget, and the resources required to do this work until\nsuch time that sufficient fees were collected/provided to the\nDepartment from the accredited institutions. Without this additional\nstaff and resources, the Department will not be able to perform these\nadditional duties necessary to be an accrediting agency authorized by\nthe U.S. Department of Education.\n\n BUDGETARY IMPLICATIONS OF THE BILL :\n\nUnknown.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
