    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 242\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 6780, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to establishing\n      the  board  of  regents  institutional  accreditation  account  to\n      provide  the framework for the state education department to spend\n      monies  received  and/or  revenues  generated  for   institutional\n      accreditation  purposes;  and  to  amend  the  education  law,  in\n      relation to  services  and  functions  relating  to  institutional\n      accreditation\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  For several decades, the State Education Department has been responsi-\nble  for  accrediting institutions of higher learning in New York State.\nThis bill would authorize the Department to charge  institutions  a  fee\nfor  providing accreditation services and to spend those funds on activ-\nities relating to providing such services.\n \n  As drafted, however, this bill  has  two  technical  flaws  that  will\nprevent  it from being implemented effectively. First, the Department is\nalready authorized to collect a fee for this service. As  no  change  in\nlaw  is  required,  amending the State Finance Law may cause unnecessary\nconfusion. Second, in order for the Department  to  spend  the  fees  it\ncollects through the accreditation process, there must also be a specif-\nic  appropriation  identified  in the State budget allowing it to do so.\nCurrently, no  such  appropriation  exists.  For  these  reasons,  I  am\nconstrained to veto this bill.\n \n  However,  one of the Department's most important functions is ensuring\nthat the State's colleges  and  universities  are  properly  accredited.\nTherefore,  I  have  secured  an agreement with the Department to ensure\nthat this service continues in the current and subsequent fiscal  years,\nwithin existing state appropriations. Further, I pledge to work with the\nDepartment,  higher  education institutions, and interested stakeholders\nto determine the appropriate amount of institution-supported fees needed\nto sustain the program  thereafter,  and  to  make  recommendations  for\ninclusion in the 2018-2019 Budget.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
